# Every directory pattern in this file is anchored with a leading slash, and that is not a style
# preference. An unanchored `foo/` matches a directory named `foo` at *any* depth, including a
# Java package - and because the result is an *ignored* file rather than an untracked one, it
# never shows up in `git status`. It has bitten this repo twice: `config/` kept
# eu.nordtal.s2.paymentsbot.config out of the first commits, and `run/` kept
# eu.nordtal.s2.steward.worker.run out of the repository until the CI build failed on it.
#
# The cost of anchoring is that a build directory in a place no pattern lists is not ignored. That
# failure is loud - the files appear as untracked in `git status` - which is exactly the trade we
# want. `:checkSourcesTracked` is the net under all of this; see build-logic.

### Build output ###
/.gradle/
/*/.gradle/
/build/
/*/build/
/.kotlin/
/*/.kotlin/
!gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar

### Secrets and runtime state. This repo is public - nothing below may ever be committed. ###
gradle.properties.local
*.env
.env
bunq-config.conf

# Runtime working directories of a server started in place.
/config/
/*/config/
/run/
/*/run/

# The four servers' plugins/ folders, bound into the containers by compose.yml (SERVERS_ROOT).
# Jars steward-worker fetched and configs an operator edited - runtime state that happens to sit
# next to the source, which is the price of being able to open plugins/smp/config.yml in an editor.
/deploy/servers/
# The pack zip the devpack profile serves on localhost. Built from resource-pack/, never edited.
/deploy/pack/
# The local environment file, and it is a SECRET FILE (season-2-ops/147). It was a copy of the
# example with obvious placeholders in it until `deploy/dev init` started asking real questions;
# it now holds a generated database password, two Steward tokens and - for anybody who answers
# them - a Discord application's client secret and a bot token. Untracked is not enough on its own,
# because `git add -A` does not ask.
/deploy/dev.env
# Everything NORDTAL_DIR points at locally: worlds, jars, configs, the database's data directory.
/deploy/local/

